5. Wrapped the component with both context providers
6. Added console.log statements to track when filter actions occur
7. Made the mock implementations more robust with proper typing

This approach should work better because:
1. We're using proper React patterns instead of test mocking
2. The contexts provide the same interface that the component expects
3. We can actually see when filter actions occur through the console
4. Everything is properly typed and follows React best practices

The component should now render without the jest error and have access to all the context values it needs.

